  "comments": "<p><a href=\"https://en.numista.com/catalogue/images/5eb162e22e332.jpg\"><img src=\"https://en.numista.com/catalogue/images/miniatures/5eb162e22e332.jpg\" alt=\"\"></a><br><a href=\"https://en.numista.com/catalogue/images/5eb162ec7623a.jpg\"><img src=\"https://en.numista.com/catalogue/images/miniatures/5eb162ec7623a.jpg\" alt=\"\"></a><br><br>New version of the common side seen from 2018 onwards. Main differences:<br>&nbsp;- Thicker and shorter numeral<br>&nbsp;- Rounded edges of the numeral<br>&nbsp;- Thicker font in \"EURO CENT\"<br>&nbsp;- Thicker lines with longer ends outside of stars<br><a href=\"https://en.numista.com/catalogue/images/5ed6053f062dd.jpg\"><img src=\"https://en.numista.com/catalogue/images/miniatures/5ed6053f062dd.jpg\" alt=\"\"></a></p><p>&nbsp;</p><p>The obverse is widely mistaken as an Alpine Primrose (<i>Primula angustifolia<strong>)</strong></i>, a flower native to the United States. &nbsp;The official mint description states that “the three smallest coins in the collection are all decorated with <i><strong>alpine flowers</strong></i>\" with the “ 5 cent piece with <i><strong>primula auricula</strong></i>, the mountain cowslip.” (emphasis added). &nbsp;</p>",
